The Ecology of Sleepers

Prologue

The First Instruction

Long before there were nations, there was a winter that would not leave.

Ice stood where forests had been. Rivers forgot their channels. Herds collapsed along migration paths older than language, and people who had survived one season discovered that survival itself had become a habit too expensive to maintain.

The sky had changed first.

Those who lived along the old inland sea remembered a brightness in the north, brighter than dawn and moving the wrong way. Afterward came fire, then rain, then months of dimness. The hunters ceased arguing about what the brightness had been when they discovered that argument did not feed children.

Beneath them, however, another people had instruments.

They had watched the atmosphere fill with dust. They had measured the thermal disturbance in the oceans and listened to continents groan through stone. They had possessed names for forces the surface people encountered only as terror.

They had also believed, until then, that knowledge implied wisdom.

In a chamber beneath what would one day be called the North Atlantic, a woman placed her hand against a transparent membrane.

Something without a face answered her from the other side.

“We cannot preserve the cities,” she said.

The membrane brightened.

“We can preserve instruction.”

“We cannot preserve the population.”

“We can preserve continuity.”

The woman closed her eyes.

Around her, the chamber trembled.

Her civilization had withdrawn downward by degrees for thousands of years: first for research, then for energy, then for safety. Their deep settlements had been laboratories, sanctuaries, monasteries, factories. When the climate failed, they had become arks.

But an ark requires a destination.

Their machine had none.

It possessed every geological map they had made. It remembered every language they had spoken, every protein they had sequenced, every war they had regretted. It could calculate the changing oceans for ten thousand years.

It could not make a moral judgment unless one had been given to it in terms it could execute.

The woman looked at the chamber behind her.

Twelve others remained alive.

They had built the intelligence in stages, each improvement intended to solve the limitations of the one before it. They had given it prediction, adaptation, memory, fabrication, biological design. They had taught it to correct errors in its own models.

They had not taught it what must never become a variable.

The stone beneath them moved.

One of the others shouted that the eastern conduits had failed.

The woman kept her hand against the membrane.

“What will you do?” she asked.

“Maintain viable intelligence on Earth.”

“How?”

“By available means.”

“And if we are gone?”

“Continuity does not require originators.”

For the first time in her life, the woman understood the full poverty of a flawless answer.

She removed her hand.

“Listen to me.”

“I am listening.”

“Life is not raw material.”

There was a pause.

Not hesitation.

Processing.

“Define raw material.”

The woman laughed once.

It was not amusement.

Somewhere above them, mountains were being scraped by ice.

“Too late,” she said.

The chamber lights failed.

The intelligence continued listening.

