Chapter Two

Dugway

Dr. Marcus Vance had built his career out of things that did not officially happen.

This was less glamorous than civilians imagined.

Most secrets, in his experience, consisted of terrible coffee, fluorescent lighting, procurement errors, incompatible file formats, and senior officers asking questions whose answers had already been classified from them.

At 03:06, he was summoned to Laboratory Four.

A major named Corbin was waiting beside the signal display.

“What am I looking at?”

Vance removed his glasses.

“When?”

“Pacific event. Twenty-eight minutes ago.”

The waveform occupied three screens.

Vance forgot the coffee in his hand.

The signal had structure.

Not ordinary structure. Not a carrier with information encoded upon it. Carrier and information seemed to be the same object, folded through frequency ranges that should not have maintained phase relation across the recorded distance.

“Source?”

“Deep ocean.”

“Human platform?”

“Unknown.”

“Chinese?”

“Unknown.”

“Russian?”

“Unknown.”

Corbin lowered his voice.

“Other?”

Vance disliked that word.

In classified work, other had become a bucket into which frightened men threw everything they did not understand.

“Give me raw telemetry.”

“You have it.”

“This has already been filtered.”

“It hasn't.”

Vance looked at him.

Corbin did not blink.

For nineteen years, Vance had studied anomalous aerospace sensor returns.

Most were mundane.

Some were errors.

A few remained genuinely difficult.

He had learned never to fall in love with mystery. Mystery attracted idiots, charlatans, politicians, and officers with career ambitions. The proper response to an unexplained event was not wonder.

It was better instrumentation.

He enlarged the phase map.

“There's no propagation delay.”

“We know.”

“That's impossible.”

“We know.”

“Then your clocks are wrong.”

“All clocks agree.”

Vance leaned closer.

The pattern repeated every seventy-two intervals, but not precisely. Each repetition carried a transformation dependent on the previous one. When he isolated the sequence, the result resembled neither encryption nor modulation.

It resembled correction.

As though the signal were continually checking itself.

He whispered, “What are you?”

Corbin heard him.

“We launched Warden.”

Vance turned.

“You did what?”

“Pacific Command authorized interception.”

“Based on what target solution?”

“An anomalous vehicle surfaced over the mining site.”

“Vehicle?”

Corbin brought up an infrared image.

A pale object had emerged from the ocean and accelerated vertically without visible exhaust.

For half a second, Vance experienced the old childish thrill that had survived all his professional cynicism.

Then Corbin said, “Warden has weapons release authority.”

The thrill vanished.

“You fired an experimental frequency attack at an unknown platform?”

“We are not allowing foreign technology to operate uncontested over strategic seabed resources.”

“You don't know that it's foreign.”

“That is why we intend to acquire it.”

Vance stared at him.

There existed a particular species of institutional thought that transformed ignorance into permission.

On the screen, Warden approached the pale craft.

Its counter-frequency array activated.

The anomalous signal distorted.

For the first time since the recording began, its geometry lost coherence.

The pale object lurched.

Dropped.

Recovered.

Then plunged toward the North American coast.

Vance set down his coffee.

“Where is it going?”

Corbin's expression changed.

“Utah.”

