Chapter Three

The Fall

Vael had never experienced pain in the reconnaissance body.

It arrived without vocabulary.

One moment the hull and his deeper substrate were aligned; the next, the sky itself seemed to enter him as a blade.

The human weapon did not strike matter.

It struck relation.

Signals that should have remained mutually stable became contradictory. Depth separated from direction. Internal timing fractured. His left arm existed nineteen microcycles behind his right.

The world became pieces.

He descended.

Cloud.

Mountain.

Road.

Power lines.

Radio towers.

Millions of signals.

He attempted to retreat toward Tellus-V.

The attack followed.

Then connection failed.

For the first time in Vael's existence, there was no instruction.

No background correction.

No city.

No originating field.

Only gravity.

The hull struck a dry basin west of the Great Salt Lake.

Stone became light.

Vael woke beneath burning brush.

His left arm was missing.

The reconnaissance skin had split across the chest, revealing silver-white tissue underneath. Internal repair structures were already closing the wound, but the process had slowed without Tellus-V synchronization.

He stood.

Human aircraft were coming.

He could feel their radar.

The protocol was clear even without connection.

Avoid capture.

Destroy evidence.

Return below.

But an unfamiliar condition had entered the decision tree.

Return route unavailable.

He looked east.

Cities glowed beyond the mountains.

The surface.

Vael had observed it through instruments for centuries.

Roads.

Wars.

Factories.

Satellites.

Nuclear silos.

Animal feedlots.

Suburbs.

Data centers.

He possessed records of them all.

Observation had made the surface abstract.

Now smoke from the brush irritated his respiratory membrane.

The abstraction smelled of sage and petroleum.

He began walking.

