Kaelen Ward woke before dawn because someone was crying inside the electrical grid.

She sat upright.

The room was dark.

Her phone lay on the floor where she had thrown it hours earlier.

The sound continued.

Not through her ears.

Through something she had spent twenty-nine years learning not to discuss.

At six, she had told her mother that streetlights hummed in different moods.

At nine, she had known a transformer was about to fail before it exploded.

At fourteen, she could stand beneath cellular towers and distinguish traffic load by sensation.

Doctors had used several words.

Auditory processing anomaly.

Synesthesia.

Seizure aura.

Anxiety.

At seventeen, after a hospital stay she refused ever to discuss, Kaelen learned the survival value of silence.

She became an audio restoration technician because broken signals were easier to live with when other people admitted they were broken.

Now the entire northern horizon seemed to be calling her.

Not language.

Pain.

She got out of bed.

The sensation sharpened.

Something had fallen.

Something was alone.

Kaelen dressed.

She did not know where she was going until she reached the highway.

Then she drove west.

