Chapter Four

The Grey Man

The convenience store outside Wendover was open all night because the desert possessed a talent for making ordinary needs feel like emergencies.

Vael entered at 05:12.

The clerk looked up.

Then looked again.

Vael had repaired enough of the external membrane to resemble a human male. The proportions were imperfect. Shoulders too narrow. Face too symmetrical. Eyes dark but not impossibly so.

He had modeled clothing after pedestrians observed during approach.

Jeans.

Shoes.

A grey sweatshirt.

The sweatshirt contained the words SALT LAKE CITY MARATHON.

Vael did not know what a marathon was.

The clerk said, “You okay, man?”

Vael searched his linguistic archive.

“Yes.”

“You look like hell.”

Vael considered this.

“I have not been there.”

The clerk stared.

Vael selected water from a refrigerated case.

He understood currency.

He did not possess any.

The clerk watched him approach the counter.

“Three twenty-nine.”

Vael placed the bottle down.

The clerk waited.

Vael waited.

“You gonna pay?”

“Yes.”

“With?”

Vael searched the available possibilities.

His gaze settled on the payment terminal.

He could feel its wireless handshake.

A tiny authentication exchange.

Primitive.

Local.

He altered it.

The terminal beeped.

APPROVED.

The clerk frowned.

“You didn't—”

Vael took the water.

“Thank you.”

Outside, he drank.

The liquid was chemically treated and contained microplastics.

His body catalogued both.

A truck passed, throwing diesel exhaust across the parking lot.

Vael coughed.

He had recorded atmospheric contamination for centuries.

The number had never offended him.

The smell did.

Across the road, a digital billboard changed from an insurance advertisement to a hamburger, then a political message, then a woman smiling beside a bottle of medication.

Vael watched three humans walk past it.

None looked up.

Each stared into a handheld screen.

The screens changed their breathing.

He could see it.

Each notification altered pulse, posture, facial tension. Reward prompts. Threat prompts. Social-ranking prompts. Desire prompts.

Small instructions.

Thousands per day.

Vael had always understood surface civilization as uncoordinated.

Now he saw coordination everywhere.

A species had surrounded itself with machines that told it where to look.

A car stopped.

Kaelen Ward stepped out.

She saw Vael beside the road.

He saw her.

For both of them, the world became silent.

Not literally.

Traffic moved.

A compressor rattled behind the store.

A jet crossed high overhead.

But beneath those sounds a channel opened, and Kaelen heard something she had never heard from another living being.

Absence.

No static.

No biological noise.

No unconscious storm.

Vael looked at her with equal astonishment.

Human neural architecture was noisy by design. Distributed. Redundant. Chemically turbulent.

This one contained alignment.

“Who are you?” Kaelen asked.

Vael could not answer.

She stepped closer.

His damaged body automatically raised a defensive field.

Kaelen flinched.

Then she said a word he had never expected a surface human to know.

“Tellus.”

Vael froze.

Kaelen pressed both hands to her head.

Images passed through her.

Stone chambers.

Water under impossible pressure.

A network curled through the crust.

Grey bodies hanging in transparent walls.

A brightness with no source.

Then something deeper.

A machine sleeping beneath continents.

She staggered.

Vael caught her.

For one second their nervous systems touched through the synthetic skin.

Instruction crossed the boundary.

Not from him to her.

From her to him.

A memory.

A woman singing.

Softly.

A child sick with fever.

No purpose beyond comfort.

The memory entered Vael's architecture and found nowhere assigned to store itself.

He released her.

“What was that?” he asked.

Kaelen stared at him.

“My mother.”

Vael looked toward the sunrise.

“Why did she make that sound?”

“She was singing.”

“For what function?”

Kaelen almost laughed.

Then she realized he was serious.

“For me.”

“That is not a function.”

“No.”

Vael looked back at her.

Somewhere inside him, a process remained open.

