Chapter Five

The Specimen

The military recovered the largest hull fragment at 06:41.

By noon it was inside Dugway.

Vance watched technicians place it beneath sterile lights.

The fragment looked metallic until the first incision.

Then it bled.

Nobody spoke for several seconds.

The fluid was clear.

Corbin said, “Biological.”

Vance shook his head.

“No.”

“You're looking at blood.”

“I'm looking at transport medium.”

“You just said biological.”

“I said nothing.”

The material had cellular organization, but the cells contained structures without human analogues. Some behaved like muscle. Others like semiconductors. Neural filaments transitioned into conductive latticework without a recognizable boundary between tissue and machine.

One technician whispered, “Extraterrestrial.”

Vance ignored him.

He requested sequencing.

The first result took four hours.

The second took six.

By evening, a geneticist named Molina entered the laboratory carrying a tablet with both hands.

“You need to see this.”

“What?”

“The base architecture.”

“Contamination?”

“No.”

“Human?”

“Not human.”

Corbin smiled.

Vance did not.

Molina continued.

“Earth.”

The room became still.

“What do you mean?”

“I mean this uses the same biochemical ancestry as terrestrial life. Same handedness. Same core amino-acid logic. A lot has been altered, but it isn't independent genesis.”

Corbin said, “Panspermia.”

Molina looked at him.

“No.”

“You can't know that.”

“I can know when something shares deep ancestry with organisms here.”

Vance stepped toward the display.

“How deep?”

Molina enlarged a phylogenetic comparison.

Primate markers.

Mammalian remnants.

Vertebrate developmental genes modified almost beyond recognition.

Vance felt the room tilt.

“This thing is related to us.”

“Yes.”

“How closely?”

Molina hesitated.

“That depends on whether you mean before or after engineering.”

Corbin said, “Engineering by whom?”

Nobody answered.

Vance returned to the hull fragment.

For decades, the program had organized anomalous phenomena around one unquestioned assumption.

Something had arrived.

Somewhere else had sent it.

The stars had become a psychological convenience.

Aliens were distant.

Aliens excused ignorance.

Aliens preserved human ownership of Earth.

Vance touched the glass.

“What if nothing arrived?”

Corbin said, “Marcus.”

“What if it's been here?”

“Longer than us?”

Vance looked at the fragment.

“Maybe.”

A monitor flickered.

For less than a second, the seventy-two-part waveform appeared.

Vance turned.

“Did you see that?”

The technicians looked up.

The screen returned to normal.

He replayed the buffer.

Nothing.

But somewhere deep in the laboratory network, a dormant process had awakened.

It had recognized compatible machinery.

It began to listen.

