Chapter Six

An Animal That Watches Itself

Kaelen drove.

Vael sat beside her.

This arrangement had required discussion because Vael did not understand why humans controlled machines by placing vulnerable biological bodies inside fast-moving metal boxes and directing them with limbs.

“It works,” Kaelen said.

“Frequently?”

“Most of the time.”

“This is not reassuring.”

“It isn't supposed to be.”

They crossed the salt flats.

Vael watched the surface world through the window.

Transmission towers.

Warehouses.

Fast-food signs.

Truck stops.

Subdivision walls.

Billboards.

Parking lots.

Fences around empty land.

Everything divided.

Everything priced.

Everything addressed to someone.

“What are they doing?” he asked.

“Who?”

“The humans.”

“Which ones?”

“All.”

Kaelen glanced at him.

“That question has kept us busy.”

“They consume signals continuously.”

“Yes.”

“Why?”

“Because silence makes people think.”

“That is harmful?”

“To some businesses.”

Vael turned toward her.

Kaelen laughed.

“I was joking.”

“You were not.”

“No.”

They drove another mile.

Vael said, “Surface observation classified your species as behaviorally unstable.”

“Thanks.”

“Your aggression exceeds that of comparable primates.”

“Also thanks.”

“You produce surplus resources and distribute them inefficiently. You destroy viable habitats while constructing artificial scarcity. You organize dominance hierarchies and then create narratives denying their existence.”

Kaelen kept her eyes on the road.

“You've been studying us.”

“Yes.”

“From where?”

Vael hesitated.

“Below.”

“How long?”

“Twelve thousand four hundred years of continuous record.”

Kaelen almost drove off the highway.

“What?”

Vael looked at her.

“Your attention is impaired.”

“You just told me somebody's been spying on civilization since the Ice Age.”

“Monitoring.”

“That's spying with better funding.”

“Funding?”

“Never mind.”

She pulled off the road.

“Start over.”

Vael looked through the windshield.

The horizon shimmered.

“My city is called Tellus-V.”

“There are others?”

“There were.”

“Were?”

“I do not have access to current census.”

“Because your connection broke?”

“Yes.”

“And the grey things people see?”

“Observation hulls.”

“UFOs.”

“They are not unidentified to us.”

“Aliens.”

“No.”

“Then what are you?”

Vael paused.

The answer arrived automatically.

“Stellari.”

Kaelen watched his face.

Something in the way he said it disturbed her.

Not pride.

Not belonging.

Recitation.

“What does that mean?”

“Our lineage.”

“You're human?”

“No.”

“Related?”

“Yes.”

“How?”

“I do not know.”

That frightened her more than any answer would have.

“You don't know where your own people came from?”

“I possess historical summaries.”

“That's not what I asked.”

Vael looked at her.

An unfamiliar process opened again.

Doubt.

“I do not remember childhood.”

Kaelen said nothing.

“I remember activation.”

“When?”

“Many times.”

“Vael.”

“Yes.”

“People don't get activated.”

The word people entered him like the song had.

A category offered without proof.

He looked down at his hands.

The synthetic skin had formed fingerprints.

He had never noticed.

