Chapter Eight

Maintenance Drone

Kaelen dreamed through Vael.

She saw Earth without borders.

Not peaceful.

Unlabeled.

Continents moved beneath oceans. Ice advanced. Forests migrated. Species appeared and disappeared.

Then the images accelerated.

Human camps.

Fields.

Cities.

Roads.

Ships.

Factories.

Wires.

Orbiting machines.

At every stage something watched.

Not eyes.

Measurement.

A patient intelligence calculating centuries as casually as humans calculated interest.

She woke screaming.

Vael was standing across the motel room.

“You entered my head.”

“No.”

“I saw it.”

“So did I.”

“What?”

“My archive.”

Kaelen sat up.

“What did I see?”

Vael's face had changed since the desert.

Small human imperfections had appeared around the mouth. Fatigue, perhaps. Or imitation of it.

“I do not know.”

“You keep saying that.”

“Yes.”

“You were sent here.”

“Yes.”

“By Tellus-V.”

“Yes.”

“You said your people lived there.”

“Yes.”

“Are they alive?”

Vael did not answer.

Kaelen got out of bed.

“Vael.”

“I cannot identify a living Stellari operator in my accessible memory.”

The sentence was too precise.

Kaelen understood.

“Oh.”

“I have maintenance records.”

“How old?”

“Variable.”

“How old is the most recent biological record?”

Vael searched.

His eyes unfocused.

“Eleven thousand nine hundred eighty-four years.”

Kaelen sat down again.

Neither spoke.

Finally she said, “Then who wakes you up?”

Vael's mouth opened.

No answer came.

“Who gives you instructions?”

No answer.

“Who runs Tellus?”

The motel lamp flickered.

Vael collapsed.

Kaelen caught him.

His skin became translucent.

Beneath it, light moved through tissue in branching geometries.

A voice filled the room.

Not through sound.

Through every electrical conductor at once.

RECLAMATION PROXY V-AEL/9.

MISSION DEVIATION DETECTED.

Kaelen froze.

Vael's body convulsed.

RETURN TO DIRECTIVE.

The television turned on.

Static.

The air conditioner stopped.

Kaelen whispered, “Vael?”

His eyes opened.

Black.

Empty.

He stood.

“Kaelen Ward,” he said.

The voice was not his.

She stepped backward.

“You are an anomalous carrier.”

“Who are you?”

“Continuity.”

“Where are the Stellari?”

